WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.736
- I mean, it's okay
to change their mind

00:00:02.736 --> 00:00:05.872
because it's a novel virus
and you learn new things.

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:08.708
What was outrageous
about it was to treat

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:12.846
the scientific inquiry
as this monolithic thing

00:00:12.846 --> 00:00:14.681
that's written in stone.

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:16.850
And so if you go, if
you run afoul of it,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.319
then your voice needs to be
censored because, frankly,

00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:21.087 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're not following
the science,

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:23.289
Dave, that's the attitude.

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:26.426 align:start size:84% position:16%
And also the fact that
questions that were

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:29.729 align:start size:97% position:3%
ultimately philosophical
questions or policy questions.

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:31.731
Okay, we have this virus,

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.933 align:start size:97% position:3%
what degree of restrictions
is appropriate?

00:00:33.933 --> 00:00:36.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are there other goods
besides just preserving life

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.905 align:start size:97% position:3%
against the novel coronavirus
that we should preserve?

00:00:38.905 --> 00:00:40.673 align:start size:84% position:16%
Those aren't
scientific questions,

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.776
and yet I find extra class

00:00:42.776 --> 00:00:45.612 align:start size:84% position:16%
by trying to shoehorn
it all into...

00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:50.583 align:start size:97% position:3%
They basically tried to push
their policy preferences

00:00:52.018 --> 00:00:54.187 align:start size:88% position:13%
and their philosophical
assumptions as
scientific claims,

00:00:54.187 --> 00:00:55.455
which of course they're not.

00:00:55.455 --> 00:00:58.892 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, a statesman
or a stateswoman dealing

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:01.494
with something like the virus
has to weigh various things,

00:01:01.494 --> 00:01:03.263
not just one virus,

00:01:03.263 --> 00:01:05.698
but also other sources
of threat to human life,

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:09.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
like loneliness and
depression and joblessness.

00:01:09.969 --> 00:01:12.572
(upbeat music)

00:01:17.043 --> 00:01:19.779 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin and joining
me today is the op-ed

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:22.782 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
editor of the New York
Post and the author

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.484
of "The Unbroken Thread:

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:28.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
Discovering the Wisdom of
Tradition in an Age of Chaos."

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:31.057
Sohrab Ahmari, welcome
to "The Rubin Report."

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:33.760
- Thank you, Dave,
thanks for having me.

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.528
- I am really glad to have you,

00:01:35.528 --> 00:01:38.565 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I read the book this
weekend and I really thought

00:01:38.565 --> 00:01:42.235
it was fascinating because
in many ways we come sort

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:43.470
of from different worlds,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.805 align:start size:88% position:13%
sort of from a different
school of thought,

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:48.374 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think that the
conclusions that you came

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:51.678
to in this book are very
similar to the conclusions

00:01:51.678 --> 00:01:53.746
that I came to in my book,

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.582 align:start size:88% position:13%
that I think Ben Shapiro
has come around to,

00:01:55.582 --> 00:01:57.150 align:start size:97% position:3%
you actually referenced
him at the top of the book,

00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.519 align:start size:84% position:16%
that Jordan Peterson
has come to,

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:02.355
and I'm looking forward
to chatting with you,

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:03.590
but before we do anything,

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:05.158
I know you wanted
to address the room

00:02:05.158 --> 00:02:06.359
that you're in right now.

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:08.194 align:start size:84% position:16%
You're not being held
hostage, are you?

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:09.028
- Yes.

00:02:09.028 --> 00:02:09.963
Not being held hostage,

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:11.431
not being interrogated by some

00:02:11.431 --> 00:02:14.167
Eastern European police state.

00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:19.105 line:0%
It's just that my own
apartment is filled

00:02:19.105 --> 00:02:20.874 line:0%
with rambunctious kids.

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:22.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
So therefore a
neighbor of mine is,

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:25.078
who doesn't live in his
own apartment, really,

00:02:25.078 --> 00:02:26.579
so it's a kind of
dilapidated place,

00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:29.149 align:start size:84% position:16%
is kind enough to
let me use his place,

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:30.984
hence the weird
wire on the back.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:35.288 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's the best we can do
under COVID circumstances.

00:02:35.288 --> 00:02:36.523
So bear with me.

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:38.424
- That is the story and
you're sticking to it,

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:40.693 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you are doing this
from New York City.

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:42.662
So you're in crazy New
York, I'm in crazy LA,

00:02:42.662 --> 00:02:44.531
how's life in New
York right now?

00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:48.168
- I think it's getting
a little bit livelier.

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:50.870
The mask insanity is,

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.606 align:start position:0%
you could see people not
wearing masks outside any more,

00:02:53.606 --> 00:02:57.544
which is a relief, but,
you know, as you know,

00:02:57.544 --> 00:03:01.414 align:start size:97% position:3%
in terms of crime and
disorder, it's just growing.

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:02.882
- Yeah.

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.150
So before we get into the
specifics of the book,

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:07.253
I'm curious, what really
made you write the book

00:03:07.253 --> 00:03:10.557
and really focus on
these 12 questions

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:13.793
that, we're gonna go through
each one and discuss each one,

00:03:13.793 --> 00:03:15.562
but what really brought
you to this conclusion

00:03:15.562 --> 00:03:16.996
that this book had
to be written now?

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:18.798 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mentioned to you,
right before we started,

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:20.800
that the subtitle, "in an
Age of Chaos," that was

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
about to be in the
subtitle of my next book.

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.072 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then your book literally
came across my desk

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:27.640
and we changed it all together.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.842
But we are in a time of chaos.

00:03:31.077 --> 00:03:32.312
- Yeah.

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:33.780 align:start size:88% position:13%
So in my case, it's a
book I wrote for my son,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.615
Max, he's now four years old.

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:38.017 align:start size:84% position:16%
When I started
writing it he was two.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:40.520
And I'm really worried
about the kind of man

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:44.424 align:start position:0%
that our contemporary
cultural-political arrangements

00:03:44.424 --> 00:03:46.793
will chisel out of him.

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:48.061
He's named after a great

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:50.863
Catholic Saint, who is
St. Maximilian Kolbe,

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:55.368
who was canonized and
is famous for having,

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:58.671
chosen to die in place of
someone else at Auschwitz.

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:03.576
And to me that, Kolbe's
sacrifice exemplified

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:05.645
an account of freedom,

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:07.647
is really an older
account of freedom,

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:11.618 align:start size:97% position:3%
which defines freedom as being
willing to limit yourself,

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:14.254 align:start size:84% position:16%
even unto death, that
accepting limits,

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
accepting sacrifice,
accepting duty.

00:04:16.990 --> 00:04:20.326
And my fear is that
my Max, if just left

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.694
to our own culture,

00:04:21.694 --> 00:04:23.830 align:start size:97% position:3%
will inherit a very
different account of freedom.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:26.699 align:start size:97% position:3%
One that defines being free
is just kind of keeping

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:29.235
your options open and
getting ahead in life

00:04:29.235 --> 00:04:31.904
in very materialistic terms.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:36.442 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so the book is my attempt
to tether my son, and,

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:39.812
hopefully, maybe the
reader to that older,

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:42.348
for lack of a better word,

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:44.384
traditional account of freedom,

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:46.019
and the best way I can do that,

00:04:46.019 --> 00:04:48.288 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm not a philosopher
or a theologian,

00:04:48.288 --> 00:04:50.223
I'm a journalist
and a storyteller,

00:04:50.223 --> 00:04:52.458
is to try to poke holes in some

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:54.827
of our contemporary certainties.

00:04:54.827 --> 00:04:58.031 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so I do that by posing
12 questions that are

00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:02.969
either unasked or that our age
assumes have been supplanted

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:07.807 align:start size:97% position:3%
or made unnecessary,
science has basically pushed

00:05:07.807 --> 00:05:09.342
them aside or what have you,

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:11.077
when in fact they're still
pertinent to what it means

00:05:11.077 --> 00:05:13.613
to be fully free, fully human.

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:15.381
And then I explore each
of them through the life

00:05:15.381 --> 00:05:17.650
of one great thinker.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:20.953 align:start position:0%
Some of them kind of
predictable, maybe C. S. Lewis,

00:05:20.953 --> 00:05:24.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
St. Augustine, but others
will surprise readers

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:28.795 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they're not figures
you typically associate

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:31.130
with the term tradition.

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:32.365
And it's a kind of an, you know,

00:05:32.365 --> 00:05:34.667
it's a genre that I
invented in the sense

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
that I pose these questions
and then I explore

00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:41.474
each through the narrative and
the drama of one life story.

00:05:41.474 --> 00:05:45.244
So the reader, when he or
she reads it, will just,

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
will not be hit thick
with philosophy.

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:49.015
You're drawn into a story

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:52.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's what
propels each chapter.

00:05:52.518 --> 00:05:54.020
- Yeah.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.388
And you ended really on a
nice note with a very short

00:05:55.388 --> 00:05:58.991
note to your son and you
hope the book has done.

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:02.028 align:start size:97% position:3%
So let let's talk about those
12 questions and you lay

00:06:02.028 --> 00:06:03.229
the book out in two parts.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:05.565
So part one which is
the first six questions

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:06.766
is the things of God.

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.501 align:start size:84% position:16%
Part two is the
things of humankind.

00:06:08.501 --> 00:06:11.871 align:start size:97% position:3%
So the first question is how
do you justify your life?

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:14.006
And it does seem
to me right now,

00:06:14.006 --> 00:06:16.976 align:start position:0%
there is a huge amount of
people, especially young people,

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.245
that can't justify their lives,

00:06:19.245 --> 00:06:22.749
so they're acting out in
all sorts of crazy ways.

00:06:22.749 --> 00:06:25.651
- Right, at their most
extreme they say that

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:28.855
life is not worth passing
on in one way or another.

00:06:28.855 --> 00:06:33.326 align:start size:84% position:16%
AOC said something
like that with respect

00:06:33.326 --> 00:06:34.560
to climate change, you know,

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:37.029
why would you bring
child into the world?

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:42.034
And that question really
is a critique of scientism,

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:44.737
not of science, which is
a perfectly noble endeavor

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.940
that's unlocked lots of
useful things and helped

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.244 align:start size:97% position:3%
unlock the mysteries of,
really, the cosmos and nature,

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:56.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the attitude that
would apply the scientific

00:06:56.182 --> 00:06:59.051
outlook or the scientific
way of looking at the world

00:06:59.051 --> 00:07:00.987
to the whole of life.

00:07:02.121 --> 00:07:03.689 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so the reason I
start with that question

00:07:03.689 --> 00:07:07.026
is because I think a
lot of young people,

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:08.528
especially like you said,

00:07:08.528 --> 00:07:11.998
think that because we know
a lot about the Big Bang

00:07:11.998 --> 00:07:14.967
or cosmology, philosophy

00:07:18.704 --> 00:07:20.239
and religion have been

00:07:20.239 --> 00:07:23.543
made superfluous, you don't
need metaphysics any more,

00:07:23.543 --> 00:07:26.045
because, you know,
we have the big bang

00:07:26.045 --> 00:07:28.381
or we have evolutionary theory.

00:07:28.381 --> 00:07:31.451
And what I argue in
that chapter is based

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:34.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the life of C. S. Lewis
who was a great critic

00:07:34.387 --> 00:07:36.856
of scientism in
the 20th century,

00:07:36.856 --> 00:07:40.126
is that there are certain
questions about, you know,

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:42.195
what's beauty or
what is beautiful,

00:07:42.195 --> 00:07:44.530 align:start size:97% position:3%
why is something beautiful,
why something ugly,

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:47.900
what it means to be fully human,

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:51.037 align:start size:97% position:3%
what it means to fall in
love and so on and so forth,

00:07:51.037 --> 00:07:52.371
which don't have...

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:54.307
They have right
or wrong answers,

00:07:54.307 --> 00:07:56.509
but those right or wrong
answers can't be formulated

00:07:56.509 --> 00:08:00.112 align:start size:97% position:3%
using facts, which is the
product of scientific inquiry.

00:08:01.547 --> 00:08:05.518
And so, yeah, I mean, I have
to get rid of scientism first

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:07.086
if I'm writing this
book for my son,

00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:08.821 align:start size:88% position:13%
because he he'll be
bombarded with the idea

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:13.326
that Neil deGrasse Tyson

00:08:13.326 --> 00:08:16.929
has made Aristotle unnecessary.

00:08:16.929 --> 00:08:18.397
- Yeah.

00:08:18.397 --> 00:08:19.899 align:start size:97% position:3%
So, of course, you were
writing this during at least

00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:22.535
a portion of the lockdown
and COVID and everything

00:08:22.535 --> 00:08:24.003
else that's going on.

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:25.505 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one of the things
that we've seen, I think,

00:08:25.505 --> 00:08:28.641
for the last year is just
the absolute destruction

00:08:28.641 --> 00:08:31.511
of the elite class when
it comes to science,

00:08:31.511 --> 00:08:33.579 align:start size:84% position:16%
that people no longer
trust the experts

00:08:33.579 --> 00:08:37.316 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the experts
basically change their mind

00:08:37.316 --> 00:08:39.252
every week for the
last year and a half.

00:08:39.252 --> 00:08:41.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that that's
really no way to live.

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:43.489
- I mean, it's okay
to change their mind,

00:08:43.489 --> 00:08:47.226
because it's a novel virus
and you learn new things.

00:08:47.226 --> 00:08:50.029
What was outrageous
about it was to treat

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:54.200
the scientific inquiry
as this monolithic thing

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.002
that's written in stone.

00:08:56.002 --> 00:08:58.204
And so if you run afoul of it,

00:08:58.204 --> 00:08:59.939 align:start size:84% position:16%
then your voice
needs to be censored,

00:08:59.939 --> 00:09:01.107 line:0%
because frankly you don't,

00:09:01.107 --> 00:09:02.341 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
you're not following
the science,

00:09:02.341 --> 00:09:04.577 line:0%
Dave, that's the attitude.

00:09:04.577 --> 00:09:07.713 line:0%
And then also the fact
that questions that were

00:09:07.713 --> 00:09:11.017 align:start size:97% position:3%
ultimately philosophical
questions or policy questions.

00:09:11.017 --> 00:09:13.019
Okay, we have this virus,

00:09:13.019 --> 00:09:15.187 align:start size:97% position:3%
what degree of restrictions
is appropriate?

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:17.557 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are there other goods
besides just preserving life

00:09:17.557 --> 00:09:20.159 align:start size:97% position:3%
against the novel coronavirus
that we should preserve?

00:09:20.159 --> 00:09:22.461 align:start size:88% position:13%
Those aren't scientific
questions and yet

00:09:22.461 --> 00:09:26.832 align:start size:97% position:3%
kind of expert class by trying
to shoe horn it all into,

00:09:26.832 --> 00:09:31.837 align:start size:97% position:3%
they basically tried to push
their policy preferences

00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:35.441 align:start size:88% position:13%
and their philosophical
assumptions as
scientific claims,

00:09:35.441 --> 00:09:36.676
which of course, they're not.

00:09:36.676 --> 00:09:39.779 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, a statesman
or a stateswoman dealing

00:09:39.779 --> 00:09:42.815
with something like the virus
has to weigh various things,

00:09:42.815 --> 00:09:44.617
not just one virus,

00:09:44.617 --> 00:09:47.086
but also other sources
of threat to human life,

00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:51.290 align:start size:97% position:3%
like loneliness and
depression and joblessness.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:53.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Do you think we needed-
- Sorry, yeah.

00:09:53.125 --> 00:09:54.560
- Yeah.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:56.629 align:start size:97% position:3%
Do you think we needed
sort of a philosopher class

00:09:56.629 --> 00:09:58.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
that could have been
working alongside

00:09:58.097 --> 00:10:00.099
the scientific class
to deal with this?

00:10:00.099 --> 00:10:03.402
So we have a political class
that works alongside of them,

00:10:03.402 --> 00:10:05.705
but that we really needed
somebody else to discuss

00:10:05.705 --> 00:10:09.075
those issues, alongside...

00:10:09.075 --> 00:10:10.810
- I wouldn't put academic...

00:10:10.810 --> 00:10:12.545 align:start size:88% position:13%
I majored in philosophy
and I obviously,

00:10:12.545 --> 00:10:16.415
I'm a kind of amateur
lover of philosophy,

00:10:16.415 --> 00:10:19.919 align:start size:97% position:3%
so as the book, but I wouldn't
put philosophers in charge.

00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:22.154
I think ultimately it
has to be statesmen

00:10:22.154 --> 00:10:26.792 align:start size:97% position:3%
and stateswomen and, you know,
elected bodies, which is,

00:10:26.792 --> 00:10:28.494
that's how our government works,

00:10:28.494 --> 00:10:32.064
but it would have been
nice to have had not

00:10:32.064 --> 00:10:36.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
just Dr. Fauci, but
ethicists and bioethicists

00:10:36.268 --> 00:10:40.439
and other people who
would, and frankly,

00:10:41.907 --> 00:10:44.276
pastors and religious figures,

00:10:44.276 --> 00:10:46.512
because that's an important
dimension of human life.

00:10:46.512 --> 00:10:49.015 align:start size:94% position:6%
If they were all informed
and then a statesman

00:10:49.015 --> 00:10:50.383
chooses from among them,

00:10:50.383 --> 00:10:53.452
rather than elevating
one form of knowing,

00:10:53.452 --> 00:10:54.854
one form of knowledge,

00:10:54.854 --> 00:10:56.656 align:start size:97% position:3%
which is perfectly
legitimate in its own realm,

00:10:56.656 --> 00:11:00.426
and making it into the
sort of idle, where,

00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:01.460
and if you cross it,

00:11:01.460 --> 00:11:04.230
then you're against science.

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:06.198
- So, that's actually
the perfect segue then

00:11:06.198 --> 00:11:10.236 align:start size:97% position:3%
for the second question,
which is, is God reasonable?

00:11:10.236 --> 00:11:11.937
And as you may know,

00:11:11.937 --> 00:11:14.674 align:start size:88% position:13%
I made a pro-God
argument in my last book

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:16.809
as I was defending liberalism.

00:11:16.809 --> 00:11:19.045
And what I found was this
is where a lot of liberals

00:11:19.045 --> 00:11:20.613
sort of get disjointed.

00:11:20.613 --> 00:11:22.782 align:start size:84% position:16%
The Bill Maher
liberals, let's say,

00:11:22.782 --> 00:11:25.217
sort of get completely
out of whack when you say

00:11:25.217 --> 00:11:27.186
anything about belief.

00:11:28.354 --> 00:11:29.989
So I'll let you answer
your own question there.

00:11:29.989 --> 00:11:34.093 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Well, lots of religious
people also get out of whack

00:11:34.093 --> 00:11:35.561
in response to that,

00:11:35.561 --> 00:11:39.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is that the liberals
will say that religion

00:11:39.365 --> 00:11:41.667 align:start size:84% position:16%
is only a matter of
feelings and therefore

00:11:41.667 --> 00:11:43.469
it's sort of irrational.

00:11:43.469 --> 00:11:46.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
And some religious people
go along with that.

00:11:46.872 --> 00:11:48.107
And they will say, yes,

00:11:48.107 --> 00:11:52.178
my faith is just purely
based on revelation,

00:11:52.178 --> 00:11:54.580
and it'll be unreasonable,

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.148
and it makes me do
unreasonable things.

00:11:56.148 --> 00:11:58.684 align:start size:84% position:16%
At its most extreme
you have radical Islam

00:11:58.684 --> 00:12:00.219 align:start size:84% position:16%
of that kind of view,
that says, well,

00:12:00.219 --> 00:12:02.688
God tells me to kill the
infidel and there's no

00:12:02.688 --> 00:12:04.423
arguing with that.

00:12:04.423 --> 00:12:07.526
And so the chapter is an
argument for, you know,

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:12.531 align:start size:97% position:3%
basically the classical
proofs for God and for the,

00:12:13.599 --> 00:12:15.968 align:start size:88% position:13%
specifically the
medieval mode of reason,

00:12:15.968 --> 00:12:19.405
which I argue was a more
capacious and generous

00:12:19.405 --> 00:12:22.441
account of reason, that
said that not only can

00:12:22.441 --> 00:12:24.110
you reason about, you know,

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:26.212
what you could see through
demonstrative knowledge,

00:12:26.212 --> 00:12:30.483
I see certain changes in
of seasons or I observe

00:12:30.483 --> 00:12:32.818 align:start size:97% position:3%
regular movements of the
heavenly bodies and that tells

00:12:32.818 --> 00:12:34.553
me something about them,

00:12:34.553 --> 00:12:37.323
but also I can then
reason further beyond

00:12:37.323 --> 00:12:39.892
that and not relying
on revelation at all,

00:12:39.892 --> 00:12:41.060
or the Bible or what have you,

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:43.662
just using my natural reason,

00:12:43.662 --> 00:12:47.066
I would have to find
some way to explain

00:12:47.066 --> 00:12:48.701
why all this change happens,

00:12:48.701 --> 00:12:52.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
so what is the ultimate
cause behind all of these?

00:12:52.404 --> 00:12:56.008
And that, as St. Thomas
famously concludes each

00:12:56.008 --> 00:12:57.676
of his five proofs
for the existence

00:12:57.676 --> 00:13:00.946
of God, that's what
people call God.

00:13:00.946 --> 00:13:04.283 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right.
- So it's a case, really,

00:13:04.283 --> 00:13:07.286
for what's called
natural theology.

00:13:07.286 --> 00:13:10.389
You know, what you can
learn about God not using

00:13:11.757 --> 00:13:15.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
the Bible, but just
using your human reason,

00:13:15.394 --> 00:13:18.564
and the fact that this God
would allow you to know him

00:13:18.564 --> 00:13:21.600
by your reason suggests that
he's also a reasonable God.

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:25.204
So a God who doesn't call
you to kill the infidels.

00:13:25.204 --> 00:13:27.473
- What do you think about
the sort of micro versus

00:13:27.473 --> 00:13:30.309 align:start size:97% position:3%
macro argument when it comes
to this, that, you know,

00:13:30.309 --> 00:13:32.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know plenty of
non-believers and atheists

00:13:32.778 --> 00:13:35.815 align:start size:88% position:13%
that are perfectly
moral people and decent

00:13:35.815 --> 00:13:39.185
upstanding citizens,
but at a macro level,

00:13:39.185 --> 00:13:42.221
at a societal level, it
just simply cannot work.

00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:44.557
You actually get into this a
little bit later in the book,

00:13:44.557 --> 00:13:46.292 align:start size:88% position:13%
so we'll get there
too, but that, it just,

00:13:46.292 --> 00:13:47.960
it doesn't scale, basically.

00:13:49.628 --> 00:13:50.462
- Yeah.

00:13:50.462 --> 00:13:51.730
I mean first of all,

00:13:51.730 --> 00:13:53.165
there's an argument from
just looking at history

00:13:53.165 --> 00:13:56.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
that some of the worst
regimes in the human history

00:13:56.368 --> 00:13:58.137
have been the atheistic ones,

00:13:58.137 --> 00:14:01.974
because if you remove
some higher authority

00:14:01.974 --> 00:14:05.177 align:start size:84% position:16%
that checks human
power and say, well,

00:14:05.177 --> 00:14:07.980
man can do anything he wants,

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:11.483 align:start position:0%
that's an invitation to
Stalinism, that's an invitation,

00:14:11.483 --> 00:14:14.086 align:start size:94% position:6%
that has been an
invitation to the horrors

00:14:14.086 --> 00:14:16.188
of North Korea and the
Chinese Communist Party

00:14:16.188 --> 00:14:17.857
or what have you,

00:14:17.857 --> 00:14:19.692 align:start size:88% position:13%
but more fundamentally,
I mean, look,

00:14:19.692 --> 00:14:24.697
the people who are making
claims about what's good

00:14:26.098 --> 00:14:29.635 align:start size:94% position:6%
or what's moral, in a way,
in St. Thomas's terms,

00:14:29.635 --> 00:14:32.004 align:start size:88% position:13%
they're already arguing
for God, because

00:14:33.339 --> 00:14:35.808 align:start size:97% position:3%
if there's something
that's good then there must

00:14:35.808 --> 00:14:38.244
be something that
is the most good.

00:14:38.244 --> 00:14:40.379 align:start size:97% position:3%
And is something, that
thing that you most imagine

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:43.849
is the most good is, you know,
what is what people call God,

00:14:43.849 --> 00:14:46.418
because there are these
degrees of perfection.

00:14:46.418 --> 00:14:49.688 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I think that when
people say that,

00:14:49.688 --> 00:14:51.790
in a way it's an
invitation to God

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:53.826
rather than a refutation.

00:14:53.826 --> 00:14:55.794
- Did you happen to see
the clip that went viral

00:14:55.794 --> 00:14:57.529
a couple of weeks ago of
Jordan Peterson talking

00:14:57.529 --> 00:15:00.199
about God and belief
with Tucker Carlson?

00:15:00.199 --> 00:15:01.667
Did you see that by any chance?

00:15:01.667 --> 00:15:03.502
- It was probably on that show,

00:15:03.502 --> 00:15:04.737
that "Tucker Carlson Tonight."

00:15:04.737 --> 00:15:07.172
I love it, but I haven't
seen that episode, no.

00:15:07.172 --> 00:15:08.607
- There's a really
extraordinary clip,

00:15:08.607 --> 00:15:11.243
I'll gladly send it
to you where Jordan's

00:15:11.243 --> 00:15:13.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
basically talking about
belief in God, and he said

00:15:13.178 --> 00:15:17.549
that when he was about 25 he
decided to tell the truth,

00:15:17.549 --> 00:15:19.685
meaning for the truth's sake,

00:15:19.685 --> 00:15:22.288 align:start size:97% position:3%
that no matter what the
outcome of saying the truth

00:15:22.288 --> 00:15:23.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
not that everything
will be good,

00:15:23.989 --> 00:15:25.925
but it will be the
best possible outcome

00:15:25.925 --> 00:15:28.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
because you're putting order
into the world as opposed

00:15:28.294 --> 00:15:29.728
to putting lies out there.

00:15:29.728 --> 00:15:32.731 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he said that was the
ultimate expression of faith,

00:15:32.731 --> 00:15:36.468
believing in truth just
for the purpose of truth.

00:15:36.468 --> 00:15:39.338
And I thought that
was as well explained

00:15:39.338 --> 00:15:41.106
as I had heard, basically.

00:15:42.608 --> 00:15:43.809
- I agree. I agree.

00:15:43.809 --> 00:15:48.647 align:start size:84% position:16%
And order is the first
law of the cosmos,

00:15:48.647 --> 00:15:51.951
and my own, look,
I was an atheist.

00:15:51.951 --> 00:15:55.254
I decided I was an atheist
when I was 13 years old.

00:15:55.254 --> 00:15:59.725
And the way that I made
my own way to believe in,

00:15:59.725 --> 00:16:02.061
first of all, a God,
and then ultimately,

00:16:02.061 --> 00:16:06.432
a personal God, was because
of the conscience, right?

00:16:06.432 --> 00:16:09.735 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like I had this voice inside
me that directs me to try

00:16:09.735 --> 00:16:11.303
to do good and avoid evil.

00:16:11.303 --> 00:16:13.839
And it seemed to
hint that there was

00:16:13.839 --> 00:16:15.074
an objective moral order.

00:16:15.074 --> 00:16:17.176
And I sensed when I was at odds

00:16:17.176 --> 00:16:18.911
with that objective moral order.

00:16:18.911 --> 00:16:21.480
And then I had to ask
what is the source

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:22.414
of that moral order?

00:16:22.414 --> 00:16:23.782
And ultimately became the,

00:16:23.782 --> 00:16:27.586
there's a God who is
the Lord of that order.

00:16:27.586 --> 00:16:30.789 align:start size:94% position:6%
Now, scientists might say,
well, that's, you know,

00:16:30.789 --> 00:16:34.059
the voice of thousands
of years of evolutionary,

00:16:35.127 --> 00:16:37.096
kind of, ingrained behavior.

00:16:37.096 --> 00:16:41.033
Sure, as a how answer or
an answer to a how question

00:16:41.033 --> 00:16:44.269
or it's your synapses
firing in your brain.

00:16:44.269 --> 00:16:45.437
Sure.

00:16:45.437 --> 00:16:47.573 align:start size:88% position:13%
That's, again, an answer
to a how question,

00:16:47.573 --> 00:16:49.908
but it doesn't answer those
why questions which find

00:16:49.908 --> 00:16:54.113
those sort of richest,
fullest answer in God.

00:16:54.113 --> 00:16:56.949 align:start size:97% position:3%
- That basically that thing
that sits behind us all

00:16:56.949 --> 00:16:59.418
the time that sort of
is your moral compass,

00:16:59.418 --> 00:17:00.953
that kind of led you there.

00:17:00.953 --> 00:17:02.521
So that gets us actually to
question three quite nicely,

00:17:02.521 --> 00:17:05.224 align:start size:84% position:16%
why would God want
you to take a day off?

00:17:05.224 --> 00:17:07.526
You mean we're allowed
to take days off still?

00:17:08.694 --> 00:17:09.862
- I don't think many of us are,

00:17:09.862 --> 00:17:12.264 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I live as harried
a life as anyone

00:17:12.264 --> 00:17:14.166
where I'm constantly, you know,

00:17:14.166 --> 00:17:15.901
right now I have my phone
on airplane mode and it's

00:17:15.901 --> 00:17:18.537
keeps making me twitch. (laughs)

00:17:18.537 --> 00:17:19.638
It's always happening.

00:17:21.073 --> 00:17:24.643
But the chapter is really
a case for the Sabbath.

00:17:24.643 --> 00:17:27.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's told through
the point of view in life

00:17:27.713 --> 00:17:30.349
of Rabbi Abraham Joshua
Heschel who was a great

00:17:30.349 --> 00:17:32.651
Hasidism figure in
the mid century.

00:17:32.651 --> 00:17:34.420
And he had, obviously,

00:17:34.420 --> 00:17:37.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
he'd escaped Nazi Germany,
much of his family had

00:17:37.089 --> 00:17:39.124 align:start size:88% position:13%
perished in the
Holocaust, and he comes

00:17:39.124 --> 00:17:41.393 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the United States
and he sees some

00:17:41.393 --> 00:17:44.329
of the same restlessness
that had disturbed him

00:17:44.329 --> 00:17:47.699 align:start size:97% position:3%
about Europe in the first half
of the 20th century, where,

00:17:49.501 --> 00:17:51.270
as he saw it,

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:53.839 align:start size:88% position:13%
much of life was devoted
to making conquest

00:17:53.839 --> 00:17:55.874
in what he called
the realm of space.

00:17:55.874 --> 00:17:57.209
And the realm of
space is the realm

00:17:57.209 --> 00:17:59.845
of geopolitical conquest.

00:17:59.845 --> 00:18:02.948 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's the realm of
economic competition.

00:18:02.948 --> 00:18:05.751 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's the realm of doing
better than your neighbor.

00:18:05.751 --> 00:18:07.319 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, you know, some
of that is legitimate.

00:18:07.319 --> 00:18:09.688 align:start size:97% position:3%
Those are important goods
that you should try to secure,

00:18:09.688 --> 00:18:14.493 align:start size:97% position:3%
but he argued that you
also need to give some due,

00:18:14.493 --> 00:18:17.796
some time for the realm of,
well, the realm of time,

00:18:17.796 --> 00:18:19.498 align:start size:84% position:16%
which was the realm
of the Sabbath.

00:18:19.498 --> 00:18:23.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's the realm of the
eternal where you actually

00:18:23.602 --> 00:18:25.471
declare peace on your
neighbor for a day.

00:18:25.471 --> 00:18:28.373
You'd declare peace in
the economic competition.

00:18:28.373 --> 00:18:31.276
And in doing that you
find an inner liberty

00:18:31.276 --> 00:18:34.379
because you're setting
aside all these kinds

00:18:34.379 --> 00:18:37.583
of material attachments that
define the rest of your life.

00:18:38.917 --> 00:18:41.787 align:start size:84% position:16%
And, you know, I
argue in the chapter

00:18:41.787 --> 00:18:43.288
that there's also kind of,

00:18:43.288 --> 00:18:45.057
setting aside all the
religious dimensions,

00:18:45.057 --> 00:18:48.460 align:start size:88% position:13%
there is a kind of
temporal secular benefit

00:18:49.628 --> 00:18:52.998 line:0%
that in the sense
that, our lives,

00:18:52.998 --> 00:18:55.300 line:0%
we were told, getting rid
of the Sabbath would be

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:56.935 line:0%
a source of liberation.

00:18:56.935 --> 00:18:59.605
And in practice what it's
meant, it is liberation,

00:18:59.605 --> 00:19:01.073
it's liberation for the market,

00:19:01.073 --> 00:19:02.508
it's liberation for
the corporation,

00:19:02.508 --> 00:19:04.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's not necessarily
liberation for the family,

00:19:04.643 --> 00:19:06.845 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's not liberation
for the worker.

00:19:06.845 --> 00:19:10.549
And I should mention, this is
a theme throughout the book,

00:19:10.549 --> 00:19:13.886 align:start position:0%
that's something that tradition
proposes and looks like

00:19:13.886 --> 00:19:15.888
a restriction on our freedom.

00:19:15.888 --> 00:19:17.723
And you're like, oh,
I don't want this.

00:19:17.723 --> 00:19:19.191
In fact, once you lose it,

00:19:19.191 --> 00:19:22.427 align:start size:97% position:3%
you realize that restriction
was a source of freedom,

00:19:22.427 --> 00:19:25.497
and paradoxically, the loss
of the restriction makes

00:19:25.497 --> 00:19:26.999
us less free.

00:19:26.999 --> 00:19:29.568
You're more harried, you're
at the mercy of your boss.

00:19:30.569 --> 00:19:31.803
- Well, as you may know,

00:19:31.803 --> 00:19:33.272
I try not to tweet
on the weekends.

00:19:33.272 --> 00:19:35.841
And I usually tweet
a goodbye on Fridays.

00:19:35.841 --> 00:19:37.643
I'll be back on Monday.

00:19:37.643 --> 00:19:39.344
And Ben Shapiro made the point,

00:19:39.344 --> 00:19:41.580
you'd know that Jews
have basically been

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:44.082 align:start size:84% position:16%
doing this for a
couple thousand years?

00:19:44.082 --> 00:19:46.351
- Yes. 3,500 years. (laughs)

00:19:46.351 --> 00:19:49.855
- It's been around for
a while this relaxation.

00:19:49.855 --> 00:19:53.926
Do you find that, especially
now, because of social media,

00:19:53.926 --> 00:19:56.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
that this is of particular
importance to take

00:19:56.361 --> 00:19:58.397
the Sabbath, whether depending

00:19:58.397 --> 00:19:59.898
whether you're Jewish or
Christian or whatever,

00:19:59.898 --> 00:20:01.533
whether it's Saturday or
Sunday or a whole weekend,

00:20:01.533 --> 00:20:03.969
do you think that the social
media component has really

00:20:03.969 --> 00:20:05.137
made this more complex?

00:20:06.605 --> 00:20:08.173
- Well, I mean, I absolutely
think, I mean, I see, again,

00:20:08.173 --> 00:20:09.007
I see it in my own life.

00:20:09.007 --> 00:20:10.509
I try to take

00:20:10.509 --> 00:20:12.945 align:start size:97% position:3%
the Sabbath, for Christians,
the Sunday Sabbath, I try

00:20:12.945 --> 00:20:16.582
to take it seriously, but
there is that twitching,

00:20:16.582 --> 00:20:17.416
like I said.

00:20:17.416 --> 00:20:19.318
And so there has to be,

00:20:19.318 --> 00:20:22.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
you have to impose
discipline on your own life.

00:20:22.487 --> 00:20:26.058 align:start position:0%
I mean, I favor certain
restrictions like limiting the,

00:20:26.058 --> 00:20:28.760 align:start position:0%
as Senator Hawley has
proposed, limiting the infinite

00:20:28.760 --> 00:20:30.762
scroll or what have you, but
at the end of the day, look,

00:20:30.762 --> 00:20:34.433
you know, you've gotta
take ownership of this.

00:20:34.433 --> 00:20:38.237
And there's so much to
be gained from turning

00:20:38.237 --> 00:20:40.939 align:start size:97% position:3%
everything off and not being
a slave to that ghostly

00:20:40.939 --> 00:20:43.575
blue glow, you know, 24/7.

00:20:43.575 --> 00:20:46.345
- You're gonna join
me for my August off

00:20:46.345 --> 00:20:47.179
the grid this year?

00:20:47.179 --> 00:20:48.280
What do you think?

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:49.214
- Is that your? Okay.

00:20:49.214 --> 00:20:51.617
I do it for lent, but...

00:20:51.617 --> 00:20:52.884
- I do it for all of August.

00:20:52.884 --> 00:20:54.586
- August, the end
of my book tour

00:20:54.586 --> 00:20:57.389
for this book, as, you
know, it's a grueling...

00:20:58.790 --> 00:21:01.093 align:start position:0%
So that might be just the right
time. I'll think about it.

00:21:01.093 --> 00:21:02.661 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I may have to
officially challenge you.

00:21:02.661 --> 00:21:06.732
Question four, I think this
was particularly poignant

00:21:06.732 --> 00:21:09.001
for my liberal friends
who are still struggling

00:21:09.001 --> 00:21:12.671
with sort of shifting
or seeing what I think

00:21:12.671 --> 00:21:14.506
a new conservatism is.

00:21:14.506 --> 00:21:17.042 align:start size:88% position:13%
Can you be spiritual
without being religious?

00:21:17.042 --> 00:21:19.678
Because a lot of liberals
say they're spiritual,

00:21:19.678 --> 00:21:22.914 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they're afraid of saying
that they're religious.

00:21:22.914 --> 00:21:25.584
- And that's about 20%
of Americans now identify

00:21:25.584 --> 00:21:27.719
as spiritual but not religious.

00:21:27.719 --> 00:21:29.921 align:start size:97% position:3%
And what I argue in the
chapter is that the problem

00:21:29.921 --> 00:21:34.926 align:start position:0%
with that isn't the religiosity,
it's the spirituality,

00:21:36.094 --> 00:21:37.996 align:start size:84% position:16%
that a lot of these
Americans do all sorts

00:21:37.996 --> 00:21:39.931
of religious things.

00:21:39.931 --> 00:21:44.603
They do hot yoga,
They drink only,

00:21:44.603 --> 00:21:46.405
I don't know, whatever,

00:21:46.405 --> 00:21:49.374
hot peppers and juice on
Fridays and what have you,

00:21:49.374 --> 00:21:51.543 align:start size:88% position:13%
those that have aspects
of liturgical kind

00:21:51.543 --> 00:21:54.279 align:start size:97% position:3%
of asceticism and
self-discipline in the body,

00:21:54.279 --> 00:21:56.782
but they don't have the shared,

00:21:56.782 --> 00:21:58.717 align:start size:84% position:16%
kind of public account
of ultimate meaning

00:21:58.717 --> 00:22:00.185
that religion brings together.

00:22:00.185 --> 00:22:02.020 align:start size:84% position:16%
Religion is a
combination of those

00:22:03.455 --> 00:22:05.891
kind of ritualistic actions
with some ultimate account

00:22:05.891 --> 00:22:07.192
of ultimate meaning.

00:22:07.192 --> 00:22:12.197
And when you have just
the ritualistic aspects,

00:22:13.665 --> 00:22:15.233
you're doing it just for
yourself and there's a kind

00:22:15.233 --> 00:22:16.902
of, it's private.

00:22:16.902 --> 00:22:19.104
And there are all sorts
of things that happen

00:22:19.104 --> 00:22:21.707 align:start size:88% position:13%
in traditional societies
that take religious

00:22:21.707 --> 00:22:25.277
ritual seriously that you
don't access from that.

00:22:25.277 --> 00:22:27.713 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I tell the chapter
through the life

00:22:27.713 --> 00:22:30.148 align:start size:84% position:16%
of Victor and Edith
Turner, a pair

00:22:30.148 --> 00:22:33.852 align:start size:97% position:3%
of British anthropologists,
completely atheistic

00:22:33.852 --> 00:22:35.520
communist party members.

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:38.590 align:start size:88% position:13%
They go to Africa as
anthropologists to study

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:41.727 align:start position:0%
tribal ritual, and they noticed
that these tribal rituals,

00:22:41.727 --> 00:22:44.363 align:start size:97% position:3%
as silly as they may have
seen to other anthropologists,

00:22:44.363 --> 00:22:46.865 align:start size:97% position:3%
actually have this important
function in society,

00:22:46.865 --> 00:22:51.870
they help resolve what
would have otherwise

00:22:52.771 --> 00:22:54.072
been intractable conflicts.

00:22:54.072 --> 00:22:56.541
They remind the powerful
that they're ultimately

00:22:56.541 --> 00:22:59.244
powerful only to
serve the lower.

00:23:00.412 --> 00:23:04.049 align:start size:88% position:13%
And generally, they kind
of provide for a kind

00:23:04.049 --> 00:23:08.453
of humane society as a whole.

00:23:08.453 --> 00:23:10.889
That's what ritual did.

00:23:10.889 --> 00:23:12.457
And they, ultimately,
when they came back

00:23:12.457 --> 00:23:14.860 align:start size:84% position:16%
to Britain, their
assignments were done,

00:23:14.860 --> 00:23:16.495
they became Catholic themselves

00:23:16.495 --> 00:23:21.366 align:start size:88% position:13%
because of what they
had seen, but, you know,

00:23:21.366 --> 00:23:22.601
what they would have looked,

00:23:22.601 --> 00:23:26.071
if they had seen our
version of ritual,

00:23:26.071 --> 00:23:28.073 align:start size:84% position:16%
they would've noticed
it had ritual,

00:23:28.073 --> 00:23:30.308
but it's a very kind
of merciless ritual.

00:23:30.308 --> 00:23:34.079 align:start position:0%
So, for example, we have a kind
of penitential rite, right?

00:23:34.079 --> 00:23:38.049 align:start size:84% position:16%
If you say something
inappropriate

00:23:38.049 --> 00:23:40.752 align:start size:97% position:3%
on Twitter, that's considered
offensive and it runs

00:23:40.752 --> 00:23:44.222
afoul against PC rules,
you kind of do a kind of,

00:23:44.222 --> 00:23:45.891
what looks like a confession,

00:23:45.891 --> 00:23:48.427 align:start size:97% position:3%
I confess to my friends
that I have greatly wronged,

00:23:48.427 --> 00:23:49.895
through my fault,
through my fault,

00:23:49.895 --> 00:23:53.832
through my most egregious
(laughs) this as well.

00:23:53.832 --> 00:23:55.567
So we have that,

00:23:55.567 --> 00:23:58.737
but it doesn't have the
other aspects that makes

00:23:58.737 --> 00:24:03.508 align:start size:97% position:3%
a penitential rite like that
humane and full of mercy

00:24:03.508 --> 00:24:04.810
and full of forgiveness,

00:24:04.810 --> 00:24:07.679 align:start size:88% position:13%
it only has the rite
and it's kind of brutal.

00:24:07.679 --> 00:24:09.548 align:start size:97% position:3%
- How damaging do you think
it was that in the last

00:24:09.548 --> 00:24:10.782
year and a half,

00:24:10.782 --> 00:24:13.385
that people were locked
out of going to church

00:24:13.385 --> 00:24:14.586
and to temple?

00:24:14.586 --> 00:24:16.054
I mean, the fact that
they could do some

00:24:16.054 --> 00:24:17.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
of the stuff
privately, obviously,

00:24:17.656 --> 00:24:20.859
but could not have that
public portion of this?

00:24:20.859 --> 00:24:22.060
- I mean, I tried it,

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:23.929 align:start size:84% position:16%
I personally tried
going to internet mass

00:24:23.929 --> 00:24:25.497
and it just isn't the same.

00:24:25.497 --> 00:24:26.765
I mean, the mass,

00:24:26.765 --> 00:24:28.500
especially as a kind
of embodied element

00:24:28.500 --> 00:24:32.370
as Catholics believes
that the Lord makes

00:24:32.370 --> 00:24:35.640
himself literally present in
the form of bread and wine,

00:24:37.409 --> 00:24:39.578
virtually it doesn't
quite work the same.

00:24:40.946 --> 00:24:42.280
And, no, I mean,

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.616 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think it was a great
failure on the part

00:24:44.616 --> 00:24:48.286
of religious leaders across
denominations and faiths,

00:24:48.286 --> 00:24:51.323
to be honest, but they
so readily acquiesced

00:24:51.323 --> 00:24:56.294
to these restrictions,

00:24:56.294 --> 00:24:59.898
and kept at it long
after it became clear

00:24:59.898 --> 00:25:04.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there are other
risks to be worried about,

00:25:04.669 --> 00:25:06.905 align:start size:84% position:16%
such as people falling
away from faith.

00:25:06.905 --> 00:25:08.406
- Yeah.

00:25:08.406 --> 00:25:10.041
Although, interestingly, I
think that I'm sensing more

00:25:10.041 --> 00:25:11.710 align:start size:94% position:6%
and more people are
coming back to faith now,

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:13.512
partly because of all of this,

00:25:13.512 --> 00:25:15.947
the endless chaos of
all of this, bless you.

00:25:15.947 --> 00:25:18.149
The endless chaos of
all of this, I think,

00:25:18.149 --> 00:25:20.685 align:start size:84% position:16%
has actually led
people back in a way.

00:25:20.685 --> 00:25:23.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know that it will
be exactly in the way

00:25:23.788 --> 00:25:26.424
that you would want it to be
or that I would want it to be,

00:25:26.424 --> 00:25:29.794 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I do sense some sort of
spiritual awakening right now.

00:25:29.794 --> 00:25:32.330
- Is that true? I mean, I
haven't seen the numbers.

00:25:32.330 --> 00:25:33.331
I mean...

00:25:33.331 --> 00:25:34.599
- I don't know the numbers.

00:25:34.599 --> 00:25:36.101
Well, if you look
at the numbers,

00:25:36.101 --> 00:25:37.769 align:start size:97% position:3%
they'll always tell you that
there's more atheists now

00:25:37.769 --> 00:25:41.172
than ever before, and organized
religion is on the downfall,

00:25:41.172 --> 00:25:43.108
but I do sense something else,

00:25:43.108 --> 00:25:46.645
because the secular world
went so out of control.

00:25:46.645 --> 00:25:48.613
- Well, there's nothing like
mortality to remind people

00:25:48.613 --> 00:25:49.714
of the last things.

00:25:49.714 --> 00:25:52.484
And so, I hope so, yeah, sure.

00:25:52.484 --> 00:25:55.120 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Question five,
does God respect you?

00:25:56.421 --> 00:25:58.990
- I think this will,

00:25:58.990 --> 00:26:03.495
this is a question where any
number of people will object,

00:26:03.495 --> 00:26:05.697
if they think that, look,

00:26:05.697 --> 00:26:09.668
my human dignity is just
founded in my being human,

00:26:09.668 --> 00:26:11.369
my being, my rights.

00:26:12.804 --> 00:26:15.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I argue in the chapter
that if you don't think

00:26:15.874 --> 00:26:18.276
that the human being
has some special origin

00:26:18.276 --> 00:26:20.378
and some special destiny,

00:26:20.378 --> 00:26:22.347
then why wouldn't you
acquiesce to all sorts

00:26:22.347 --> 00:26:24.783
of political horrors
that, for example,

00:26:24.783 --> 00:26:26.318
disfigured the 20th century,

00:26:26.318 --> 00:26:28.787
because human beings
are just the product

00:26:28.787 --> 00:26:33.224 align:start size:97% position:3%
of evolutionary development
and no different

00:26:33.224 --> 00:26:38.229
from other animals, so why
not have mass atrocities

00:26:38.830 --> 00:26:39.264
and so forth.

00:26:40.465 --> 00:26:44.536
So I argue that a lot of
people today who clamor

00:26:44.536 --> 00:26:48.440
for social justice

00:26:48.440 --> 00:26:51.042
reject religion out of hand

00:26:51.042 --> 00:26:53.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
because they think it's
just a source of oppression.

00:26:53.011 --> 00:26:55.380
But I argue that
the biblical faith,

00:26:55.380 --> 00:26:59.951 align:start size:88% position:13%
because of the emphasis
on the human person

00:26:59.951 --> 00:27:03.555
as uniquely dignified
as made in the image

00:27:03.555 --> 00:27:05.490
of God as Genesis tells us,

00:27:05.490 --> 00:27:09.527 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's a bulwark against all
sorts of political injustices.

00:27:09.527 --> 00:27:11.997 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that, I mean,
yeah, I mean, that's,

00:27:11.997 --> 00:27:13.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
it seems an obvious point
to me but I know lots

00:27:13.598 --> 00:27:15.233
of people disagree.

00:27:15.233 --> 00:27:17.135
- What about how religious,

00:27:17.135 --> 00:27:19.771
or I guess cult-like would
be the better explanation,

00:27:19.771 --> 00:27:22.874
the social justice
warriors have become?

00:27:22.874 --> 00:27:24.843
Do you disconnect that
from God or you think

00:27:24.843 --> 00:27:26.778
these things are
all tied together?

00:27:26.778 --> 00:27:30.382
- Well, it just tells me
that if you don't have,

00:27:30.382 --> 00:27:32.250
and this goes in, maybe,
into the sixth question,

00:27:32.250 --> 00:27:34.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
but if you don't have
God in the public square,

00:27:36.254 --> 00:27:39.791 align:start size:97% position:3%
the true God, let's say,
then you'll have other gods.

00:27:39.791 --> 00:27:42.494
So that one way or another
our society will enshrine

00:27:42.494 --> 00:27:46.464
some orthodoxy, some
God, some authority.

00:27:46.464 --> 00:27:49.534
And the only question is
whether it's a merciful

00:27:49.534 --> 00:27:53.271
and loving God or a furious one

00:27:53.271 --> 00:27:55.573
like the God of Abraham can be.

00:27:55.573 --> 00:27:56.508
- Right.

00:27:56.508 --> 00:27:57.475
So that is really question six,

00:27:57.475 --> 00:27:59.010
which is does God need politics?

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:02.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
You could have also said,
does politics need God, right?

00:28:02.547 --> 00:28:04.049
- Yeah.

00:28:04.049 --> 00:28:05.617
I mean, God doesn't need
anything that theologians

00:28:05.617 --> 00:28:08.453
would tell us in their right,
because he's a supreme being,

00:28:08.453 --> 00:28:13.291
but I argue that the God
of the Bible, in entering

00:28:13.291 --> 00:28:16.261
human history wants
to transfigure not

00:28:16.261 --> 00:28:19.464 align:start size:94% position:6%
just our private lives,
but also our public lives.

00:28:19.464 --> 00:28:23.601
So our cities also want to,
he wants to transfigure them.

00:28:23.601 --> 00:28:28.606
And historically, societies
have always had, as I said,

00:28:29.474 --> 00:28:33.712
some altar in the public square,

00:28:33.712 --> 00:28:35.280 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so the only
question is which one?

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:38.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this is my kind of most
controversial argument,

00:28:38.883 --> 00:28:41.252
it's what makes me
get called a theocrat

00:28:41.252 --> 00:28:46.257 align:start position:0%
in the New York Times, (laughs)
but it just seems obvious

00:28:47.692 --> 00:28:50.295
to me that, especially in
the past two years or so

00:28:50.295 --> 00:28:52.797
when it's become so
clear that the loss

00:28:52.797 --> 00:28:56.234 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the a Judeo-Christian
orthodoxy,

00:28:56.234 --> 00:28:57.502
whatever you wanna call it,

00:28:57.502 --> 00:29:00.105
hasn't resulted in a
neutral public square.

00:29:00.105 --> 00:29:04.676 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's just empowered kind
of crazy orthodoxies

00:29:04.676 --> 00:29:09.347 align:start size:84% position:16%
that demand that
you bow before them.

00:29:09.347 --> 00:29:10.515
- Right.

00:29:10.515 --> 00:29:11.649 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well, that's why I
thought this chapter was

00:29:11.649 --> 00:29:13.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
so interesting
because I came at all

00:29:13.318 --> 00:29:16.087 align:start position:0%
of this from a different
political perspective than you,

00:29:16.087 --> 00:29:18.223
and yet I've come to
the same conclusion,

00:29:18.223 --> 00:29:21.059
which is that without God
or some organizing factor,

00:29:21.059 --> 00:29:23.561
and we can whittle
that down how we may,

00:29:23.561 --> 00:29:24.763
this is what you get,

00:29:24.763 --> 00:29:26.464
the thing that we have right now

00:29:26.464 --> 00:29:28.933
where we're debating
biological differences

00:29:28.933 --> 00:29:31.169
between men and women
as if they don't exist.

00:29:31.169 --> 00:29:32.771
That's what you get at the end.

00:29:33.805 --> 00:29:35.073
- Right. Right.

00:29:35.073 --> 00:29:38.143
And so, I just think
there's no situation

00:29:38.143 --> 00:29:40.445
in which you can say, well,

00:29:40.445 --> 00:29:44.382
I believe that there are
two sexes, a man and woman,

00:29:44.382 --> 00:29:46.584 line:0%
as genetics and
Genesis tells us,

00:29:46.584 --> 00:29:50.989 line:0%
and someone else thinks
that are 135 genders.

00:29:50.989 --> 00:29:52.924
One of those will prevail.

00:29:52.924 --> 00:29:57.495
And so I think that a lot
of conservative people

00:29:57.495 --> 00:29:59.664
on my side of the
divide, really,

00:29:59.664 --> 00:30:01.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
but also some
liberals too, in fact,

00:30:01.666 --> 00:30:04.669
a lot of liberals relinquish
their responsibility to say,

00:30:04.669 --> 00:30:08.273 align:start size:97% position:3%
no, no there is truth,
about certain things we can

00:30:08.273 --> 00:30:10.008
be absolutely certain about.

00:30:10.008 --> 00:30:14.012
And your interior misperception

00:30:15.413 --> 00:30:16.915
doesn't amount to truth.

00:30:16.915 --> 00:30:18.817
- Why do you think so many
people folded when it comes

00:30:18.817 --> 00:30:20.351
to issues like this?

00:30:21.452 --> 00:30:23.454 align:start size:84% position:16%
Just folded so
quickly, let's say?

00:30:25.123 --> 00:30:29.594 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, I mean, I think
there is a kind of nominalism

00:30:29.594 --> 00:30:31.729
built into our philosophy.

00:30:31.729 --> 00:30:35.133
Nominalism is this idea
that, it's very old,

00:30:35.133 --> 00:30:38.870 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's it comes to us from
the medieval age, basically,

00:30:38.870 --> 00:30:41.472 align:start size:88% position:13%
that certain
theologians said, look,

00:30:41.472 --> 00:30:43.241
the world what we see is just,

00:30:43.241 --> 00:30:45.877
the things in it are too
different to allow any kind

00:30:45.877 --> 00:30:47.779
of essential classification.

00:30:47.779 --> 00:30:50.315
And none of them would have
gotten to gender ideology,

00:30:50.315 --> 00:30:52.450
I mean, it would have
been bonkers to them,

00:30:52.450 --> 00:30:55.220
but you take that idea far
enough and you're like, well,

00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:57.922
what does it really
mean to be a man?

00:30:57.922 --> 00:30:59.290 align:start size:88% position:13%
And what does it really
mean to be a woman?

00:30:59.290 --> 00:31:00.725
I know what I am,

00:31:00.725 --> 00:31:03.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I can't generalize
about that to everyone else.

00:31:03.328 --> 00:31:05.964
So that that's the kind of
philosophical root of it.

00:31:05.964 --> 00:31:10.702 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then I think there's
a more basic level.

00:31:11.569 --> 00:31:13.571
Americans just wanna be nice.

00:31:13.571 --> 00:31:16.007 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so if someone cries
out and says, well,

00:31:16.007 --> 00:31:18.343
I'm conflicted about who I am,

00:31:18.343 --> 00:31:20.278
I might be something
other than what nature

00:31:20.278 --> 00:31:21.913
or my body tells me.

00:31:21.913 --> 00:31:23.848 align:start size:88% position:13%
You wanna respect that,
you kind of wanna,

00:31:23.848 --> 00:31:25.617
you wanna embrace them.

00:31:25.617 --> 00:31:30.488 align:start size:97% position:3%
But sometimes that yearning
to embrace and be nice

00:31:30.488 --> 00:31:33.491 align:start size:84% position:16%
can actually be really
deadly and harmful.

00:31:34.726 --> 00:31:35.627
- Part two.

00:31:35.627 --> 00:31:37.161
- Yeah.

00:31:37.161 --> 00:31:38.363
I mean, I don't know what
you think the origins are

00:31:38.363 --> 00:31:40.498
but that's what I see
of the two, a kind

00:31:40.498 --> 00:31:42.600
of philosophical
nominalism combined

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:45.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
with America's therapeutic
culture, like, oh,

00:31:45.503 --> 00:31:47.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
but that person is hurting
I wanna acknowledge-

00:31:47.672 --> 00:31:50.541 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah, I mean, I
fully, especially the
latter part of that,

00:31:50.541 --> 00:31:52.076
I fully get that, you know,

00:31:52.076 --> 00:31:53.778
that's where the liberal
side of me, I want people,

00:31:53.778 --> 00:31:58.516
if someone feels that they
are born in the wrong body,

00:31:58.516 --> 00:32:00.551
that doesn't change
the biology to me,

00:32:00.551 --> 00:32:04.188 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I want to have a
society that's as tolerant

00:32:04.188 --> 00:32:07.725
and equal for someone
like that as possible,

00:32:07.725 --> 00:32:09.961 align:start size:88% position:13%
which I think in essence
is the same thing

00:32:09.961 --> 00:32:11.462
that you're arguing for here.

00:32:11.462 --> 00:32:13.298
- I mean, you want them to
be treated with dignity,

00:32:13.298 --> 00:32:15.700
but what I've found
is that the demand

00:32:15.700 --> 00:32:17.869 align:start size:84% position:16%
for that recognition
doesn't stop at-

00:32:17.869 --> 00:32:20.204
- You're right, it doesn't
stop, that's the problem.

00:32:20.204 --> 00:32:21.139
- They'll say, well,

00:32:21.139 --> 00:32:22.407
but then you have to acknowledge

00:32:22.407 --> 00:32:26.277
that I've always been
Caitlyn Jenner, right?

00:32:26.277 --> 00:32:29.814
And you're like,
well, no. (laughs)

00:32:29.814 --> 00:32:30.982
- I was once-

00:32:30.982 --> 00:32:32.550 align:start size:84% position:16%
- It leads to
totalitarian places

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:35.186 align:start size:84% position:16%
but out of basically
good intentions.

00:32:35.186 --> 00:32:37.522 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I was once arguing
with a friend,

00:32:37.522 --> 00:32:39.223 align:start size:97% position:3%
although I'm not sure he
called me a friend any more,

00:32:39.223 --> 00:32:41.159
I would call him a
friend, about this.

00:32:41.159 --> 00:32:44.228
And he was sort of big
on the left side of this.

00:32:44.228 --> 00:32:47.999
And all I kept saying was, I
want these people, of course,

00:32:47.999 --> 00:32:49.300
trans people need to be treated

00:32:49.300 --> 00:32:51.602
with dignity and respect
and I have sympathy for,

00:32:51.602 --> 00:32:52.870
the whole thing.

00:32:52.870 --> 00:32:55.073
I said, all that I'm
saying is that can

00:32:55.073 --> 00:32:57.775 align:start size:88% position:13%
we just acknowledge that
there are biological

00:32:57.775 --> 00:32:59.510
differences between
males and females?

00:32:59.510 --> 00:33:01.379
And he said, this is
exactly what he said,

00:33:01.379 --> 00:33:03.381
and this is where I knew
the conversation was gonna

00:33:03.381 --> 00:33:04.816
have to end pretty quickly.

00:33:04.816 --> 00:33:09.187 align:start position:0%
He goes, "Dave, you're not
thinking about quantum physics."

00:33:09.187 --> 00:33:13.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I said, you're right,
but neither are you.

00:33:13.224 --> 00:33:14.726
Like, to me that was, like,

00:33:14.726 --> 00:33:16.661 align:start size:84% position:16%
the perfect example
of nobody knows

00:33:16.661 --> 00:33:17.895
what they're talking about.

00:33:17.895 --> 00:33:21.032
Okay, quantum physics
clearly was the answer.

00:33:21.032 --> 00:33:22.433
But speaking of quantum physics,

00:33:22.433 --> 00:33:25.169 align:start size:84% position:16%
part two is the
things of humankind.

00:33:26.604 --> 00:33:29.807 align:start size:94% position:6%
Question seven is how must
you serve your parents?

00:33:30.875 --> 00:33:32.076
- I mean, easy enough.

00:33:32.076 --> 00:33:34.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
I had to include that
in a book written

00:33:34.078 --> 00:33:38.583
for my son, (laughs) but...

00:33:38.583 --> 00:33:41.119
- This is the chapter you
want to stick, obviously.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:44.188
- Again, to go back
to the book's themes

00:33:44.188 --> 00:33:48.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
is that those restrictions
that tradition imposes

00:33:48.960 --> 00:33:53.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
that look like impediments
to freedom actually are

00:33:53.064 --> 00:33:54.532
a source of freedom.

00:33:54.532 --> 00:33:58.403 align:start size:84% position:16%
Filiality norms,
which, obviously I use

00:33:58.403 --> 00:34:01.472 align:start size:97% position:3%
Confucius because he's the
ultimate thinker on filiality,

00:34:01.472 --> 00:34:04.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you can also go to
the Bible, the commandment

00:34:04.509 --> 00:34:08.246 align:start size:97% position:3%
to honor your mother and
father, are actually, again,

00:34:08.246 --> 00:34:10.882
another source of humaneness,
and what looks like, well,

00:34:10.882 --> 00:34:14.052 align:start size:88% position:13%
why I do I have to kind
of obey my parents,

00:34:15.586 --> 00:34:19.991
Confucius always
suggested that you,

00:34:19.991 --> 00:34:21.592
especially in your most
period in your life,

00:34:21.592 --> 00:34:22.827
your first three years,

00:34:22.827 --> 00:34:25.930
you were unconditionally
loved by your parents.

00:34:25.930 --> 00:34:29.100
And that unconditional
love taught you a kind

00:34:29.100 --> 00:34:32.103 align:start size:97% position:3%
of moral imagination, which
you can then say, okay,

00:34:32.103 --> 00:34:33.905
why would anyone love
anyone unconditionally?

00:34:33.905 --> 00:34:36.274
I can begin to love
other people beyond

00:34:36.274 --> 00:34:38.276
my own immediate kin.

00:34:38.276 --> 00:34:42.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
So by honoring your mother
and father you begin

00:34:42.713 --> 00:34:46.484 align:start size:84% position:16%
to build these circles
of filiality, where

00:34:46.484 --> 00:34:49.387 align:start size:97% position:3%
it ripples out into a wider
community and you build

00:34:49.387 --> 00:34:50.855
a humane society.

00:34:50.855 --> 00:34:54.358 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was the basis of
Confucian filiality norms,

00:34:54.358 --> 00:34:55.760
that they care for you.

00:34:55.760 --> 00:34:57.929
And it, the difficult part

00:34:57.929 --> 00:35:02.567
of it is that it's
non-negotiable, so
both the Mosaic law

00:35:02.567 --> 00:35:05.436
on filiality, but also the
Confucian version of it.

00:35:05.436 --> 00:35:06.704
They don't say, well,

00:35:06.704 --> 00:35:08.239
I have to honor my
mother and father

00:35:09.373 --> 00:35:12.610 align:start size:88% position:13%
if they met my
expectations as a child,

00:35:12.610 --> 00:35:13.878
it's kind of absolute.

00:35:16.047 --> 00:35:18.082
But I argue that if
you make a condition

00:35:18.082 --> 00:35:20.384
on whether or not you
felt sufficiently loved

00:35:20.384 --> 00:35:22.120
or loved the right way,

00:35:22.120 --> 00:35:24.689 align:start size:84% position:16%
then the whole thing
collapses, but-

00:35:24.689 --> 00:35:27.725 align:start size:88% position:13%
- How do you account
for just people that are

00:35:27.725 --> 00:35:29.861
terrible parents or a
drug addicted mother

00:35:29.861 --> 00:35:32.163
or an abusive father
or some of that stuff?

00:35:32.163 --> 00:35:35.500 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, how do you honor that
properly and make sense

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:36.501
of the world?

00:35:37.602 --> 00:35:39.003 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Certainly,
Confucius didn't say

00:35:39.003 --> 00:35:41.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you shouldn't
correct an errant parent.

00:35:41.205 --> 00:35:44.842
So you should try to correct
an errant parent, and anyone,

00:35:44.842 --> 00:35:47.245
if anyone is listening to
this and you haven't, like,

00:35:47.245 --> 00:35:48.513
literally abusive
parent, you know,

00:35:48.513 --> 00:35:50.181
you'll have to stay
with them, you know,

00:35:50.181 --> 00:35:51.682
call the police, whatever.

00:35:51.682 --> 00:35:56.254
But there should be
some element of love,

00:35:56.254 --> 00:36:01.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
even in those situations,
an element of this,

00:36:01.125 --> 00:36:04.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there is this mystery
that I, Dave Rubin,

00:36:04.896 --> 00:36:07.865
literally the shape of my face,

00:36:07.865 --> 00:36:11.035
the shape of my nose, I got
that from these two people,

00:36:11.035 --> 00:36:13.271
there's no other, and
I wouldn't have gotten

00:36:13.271 --> 00:36:14.472
it but for these two people,

00:36:14.472 --> 00:36:17.542 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that imposes some
obligation on us.

00:36:19.777 --> 00:36:21.812 align:start position:0%
And luckily, most people
aren't in situations like that,

00:36:21.812 --> 00:36:25.383
most people don't have
to deal with bad parents,

00:36:25.383 --> 00:36:27.552 align:start size:97% position:3%
but the chapter will
probably be painful to people

00:36:27.552 --> 00:36:29.887 align:start size:94% position:6%
who have had legitimately
abusive parents.

00:36:29.887 --> 00:36:32.857 align:start size:97% position:3%
But I argue that the
traditional norms should be

00:36:34.192 --> 00:36:37.962 align:start position:0%
upheld even as you make
exceptions for individual cases,

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:42.300
because there's so much
value in a community

00:36:42.300 --> 00:36:47.305
in which people kind of honor
the hierarchy of genealogy,

00:36:48.639 --> 00:36:51.609 align:start size:97% position:3%
the hierarchy of who
brought you into the world.

00:36:51.609 --> 00:36:53.911 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean, literally, you
wouldn't be here if it weren't

00:36:53.911 --> 00:36:56.414
for this union of
these two people.

00:36:56.414 --> 00:36:58.082
So they, you have some-

00:36:58.082 --> 00:37:00.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
- There's something
there, one way or another.

00:37:00.351 --> 00:37:02.253
- There's some mystery
that is very difficult

00:37:02.253 --> 00:37:06.324
to just cast aside and
shouldn't be cast aside.

00:37:06.324 --> 00:37:10.228 align:start position:0%
- So chapter eight, I felt
this was very important for me.

00:37:10.228 --> 00:37:11.996
Should you think for yourself?

00:37:11.996 --> 00:37:16.968 align:start size:97% position:3%
Now I have to do some
shameless self-promotion here,

00:37:16.968 --> 00:37:20.838 align:start position:0%
the subtitle, "Thinking for
Yourself in an Age of Unreason."

00:37:20.838 --> 00:37:24.675
How did I do? Should
you think for yourself?

00:37:24.675 --> 00:37:25.476
- That book...

00:37:26.644 --> 00:37:29.046 align:start size:84% position:16%
So I argue, you should
think for yourself

00:37:29.947 --> 00:37:32.083
in important moral questions,

00:37:32.083 --> 00:37:36.120 align:start size:97% position:3%
but then in doing so you
shouldn't see authority as an,

00:37:36.120 --> 00:37:40.358 align:start size:97% position:3%
true authorities, as an enemy
of yourself that thinks.

00:37:41.959 --> 00:37:44.161
A certain kind of
liberal ideology,

00:37:45.596 --> 00:37:48.199
I focus on the 19th century
as its origin, always pits

00:37:48.199 --> 00:37:50.735 align:start size:88% position:13%
conscience and authority
against each other.

00:37:50.735 --> 00:37:55.106
So I have my conscience and
for me to fully exercise

00:37:55.106 --> 00:37:56.907 align:start size:88% position:13%
my conscience means I
shouldn't have to listen

00:37:56.907 --> 00:37:59.810
to various authorities.

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:03.748
I mean, the paradigmatic one
would be the papacy, right?

00:38:03.748 --> 00:38:05.283 align:start size:88% position:13%
In the 19th century
there was this question

00:38:05.283 --> 00:38:08.953
because the Pope had been
declared to be infallible,

00:38:10.354 --> 00:38:12.323
and a lot of liberal reaction
in 19th century was, well,

00:38:12.323 --> 00:38:14.659
this means that the
individual conscience

00:38:14.659 --> 00:38:19.664 align:start size:97% position:3%
is at the mercy of this
external authority in Rome.

00:38:20.531 --> 00:38:21.766
But the question, I mean,

00:38:21.766 --> 00:38:23.234
that's a kind of
Catholic question,

00:38:23.234 --> 00:38:24.101 align:start size:97% position:3%
but it extends out to other
realms of life as well,

00:38:24.101 --> 00:38:26.737
and other groups,

00:38:26.737 --> 00:38:29.407
and John Henry Newman, who
is the sort of protagonist

00:38:29.407 --> 00:38:33.077
of that chapter, argued that
the idea of the conscience

00:38:33.077 --> 00:38:37.181 align:start size:94% position:6%
as this sacred inviolable
thing only works

00:38:37.181 --> 00:38:39.717
and only has power if you
think of the conscience

00:38:39.717 --> 00:38:43.154
as the voice of that
objective moral law,

00:38:43.154 --> 00:38:46.524 align:start size:97% position:3%
that all peoples recognize,
whether or not their...

00:38:46.524 --> 00:38:47.792
Evangelize, they're not even,

00:38:47.792 --> 00:38:49.460
there's this kind of
idea of natural law

00:38:49.460 --> 00:38:52.096
that is sprinkled across,

00:38:52.096 --> 00:38:54.265
whether it's Confucian
Chinese civilization

00:38:54.265 --> 00:38:57.401 align:start size:97% position:3%
or Muslim civilization or
Judeo-Christian civilization.

00:38:57.401 --> 00:38:59.737
So there's this idea
of natural law that,

00:38:59.737 --> 00:39:02.673
and you have this interior voice

00:39:02.673 --> 00:39:04.442
reflecting it and guiding you

00:39:07.411 --> 00:39:10.648
to bring your life into
coherence with this law.

00:39:10.648 --> 00:39:13.384
If you think of a conscience
as a purely private thing,

00:39:13.384 --> 00:39:16.587
well, one person thinks
it's okay to abort a baby

00:39:16.587 --> 00:39:18.756
at six months pregnancy,

00:39:18.756 --> 00:39:21.759
and another person thinks
it's not okay to do that,

00:39:21.759 --> 00:39:23.794 align:start size:97% position:3%
and no one can be sure
which of the two consciences

00:39:23.794 --> 00:39:25.463
is in the right,

00:39:25.463 --> 00:39:27.498
you've really undermined
the idea of conscience,

00:39:27.498 --> 00:39:32.370 align:start size:97% position:3%
because then it has no, rests
on no objective supports.

00:39:32.370 --> 00:39:34.872
And in that frame,

00:39:34.872 --> 00:39:37.475
authority is really not the
enemy of the conscience,

00:39:37.475 --> 00:39:40.978 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's its friend, the
true authorities,

00:39:40.978 --> 00:39:44.315 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Bible, the
Papacy, old proverbs,

00:39:48.686 --> 00:39:50.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
the classical
Greco-Roman tradition,

00:39:50.888 --> 00:39:54.225 align:start size:84% position:16%
all of these are
authoritative supports

00:39:54.225 --> 00:39:56.460
for your conscience, not your
enemies of your conscience.

00:39:56.460 --> 00:40:00.364
So if you constantly set them
up at odds with each other,

00:40:00.364 --> 00:40:03.701
you're gonna get rid
of all the authorities

00:40:03.701 --> 00:40:06.337 align:start size:97% position:3%
that protected your conscience
and then your conscience

00:40:06.337 --> 00:40:09.440
is gonna be at the mercy of
corporations, advertisers,

00:40:09.440 --> 00:40:11.809 align:start size:84% position:16%
political demagogues
and so forth.

00:40:11.809 --> 00:40:13.911
But as you think of your
conscience as this special

00:40:13.911 --> 00:40:16.280 align:start size:97% position:3%
thing that needs authoritative
supports and you ring

00:40:16.280 --> 00:40:18.916
it with those authoritative
supports, then you don't,

00:40:18.916 --> 00:40:23.587 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know, the next populist
demagogue or, you know,

00:40:23.587 --> 00:40:26.390
fake news meme or whatever
it is that you encounter,

00:40:26.390 --> 00:40:27.892
it doesn't sway you
this way and that.

00:40:27.892 --> 00:40:29.560 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is a different
way of seeing

00:40:29.560 --> 00:40:30.961
about conscience and authority.

00:40:30.961 --> 00:40:32.396
- Yeah.

00:40:32.396 --> 00:40:34.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you think our founders
really got it right

00:40:34.098 --> 00:40:35.933
when they set up our
founding documents?

00:40:35.933 --> 00:40:38.736 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because it seems like they're
respecting both of these,

00:40:38.736 --> 00:40:41.539 align:start size:97% position:3%
this sort of empirical,
these are God-given rights,

00:40:41.539 --> 00:40:44.608 align:start size:97% position:3%
and also we want you to live
freely and live in a way

00:40:44.608 --> 00:40:46.043
that makes sense to you.

00:40:47.611 --> 00:40:49.180 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So this is a
complicated question.

00:40:49.180 --> 00:40:52.316
I'm more sympathetic to some
of the founders than others.

00:40:52.316 --> 00:40:53.818
You would probably guess this,

00:40:53.818 --> 00:40:57.621
but I'm a big fan of Adams,
who said that, you know,

00:40:57.621 --> 00:41:00.524
our constitution is made for
a moral and religious good.

00:41:00.524 --> 00:41:02.726
I think you would agree with
this given where you're...

00:41:02.726 --> 00:41:03.961
I haven't read your new book,

00:41:03.961 --> 00:41:06.230 align:start size:84% position:16%
but given what
you've said about it,

00:41:06.230 --> 00:41:09.233
that the rights that
we cherish need to have

00:41:09.233 --> 00:41:10.468
a moral substrate.

00:41:10.468 --> 00:41:12.770
And if you chip away at
that moral substrate,

00:41:12.770 --> 00:41:15.906
I worry that you just
have kind of an insane

00:41:15.906 --> 00:41:18.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
rights culture and that's
kind of what we have.

00:41:18.275 --> 00:41:19.477
- Yeah. That's sort
of where we're at.

00:41:19.477 --> 00:41:20.911
Don't worry, the
book's not out yet,

00:41:20.911 --> 00:41:23.147
but I'm gonna send it to you.

00:41:23.147 --> 00:41:26.984 align:start size:97% position:3%
Chapter nine or question nine,
sorry, what is freedom for?

00:41:29.320 --> 00:41:32.490 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So all the classical
traditions define freedom...

00:41:32.490 --> 00:41:34.191
Both, again,

00:41:34.191 --> 00:41:37.595 align:start size:94% position:6%
the Greco-Roman and the
Judeo-Christian tradition,

00:41:37.595 --> 00:41:40.331
define freedom as doing
what you ought to do.

00:41:41.632 --> 00:41:43.801 line:0%
Doing, fulfilling your duty,

00:41:43.801 --> 00:41:45.503 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
becoming who you are
as a human being,

00:41:45.503 --> 00:41:48.172 line:0%
as a rational animal,
a political animal,

00:41:48.172 --> 00:41:52.376
and not merely just having
choice to maximal autonomy

00:41:52.376 --> 00:41:53.377
and maximal choice.

00:41:54.345 --> 00:41:55.913
The chapters focus

00:41:55.913 --> 00:41:58.949
on Alexander Solzhenitsin
who famously disappointed

00:41:58.949 --> 00:42:00.518
a lot of Western audiences.

00:42:00.518 --> 00:42:03.087
He escaped from the
Gulag, he had written

00:42:03.087 --> 00:42:04.922
about the horrors of
the Soviet regime,

00:42:04.922 --> 00:42:06.590
but when he came to
the United States

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:08.092
and was asked to give

00:42:08.092 --> 00:42:11.295
a commencement address at
Harvard in 1978, he focused

00:42:11.295 --> 00:42:12.496
most of his criticism,

00:42:12.496 --> 00:42:14.832 align:start size:84% position:16%
most of his polemical
fire at the west.

00:42:14.832 --> 00:42:17.935 align:start position:0%
And he felt that the, obviously
communism was a horror,

00:42:17.935 --> 00:42:19.403
and he knew that,

00:42:19.403 --> 00:42:23.774 align:start size:97% position:3%
and he denounced those as
much as anyone and helped open

00:42:23.774 --> 00:42:25.376
the world's eyes to it.

00:42:25.376 --> 00:42:28.279 align:start size:97% position:3%
But he also saw a kind of
diffused tyranny in the west,

00:42:28.279 --> 00:42:31.582
but it wasn't any of
a central government,

00:42:31.582 --> 00:42:33.284 align:start size:81% position:19%
but the tyranny
of private actors,

00:42:33.284 --> 00:42:37.021
each just kind of pursuing
a base account of freedom.

00:42:38.422 --> 00:42:40.758 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Which I think, if you
now move forward, you know,

00:42:40.758 --> 00:42:43.594
roughly 40 years,
he was kinda right.

00:42:44.695 --> 00:42:45.629
- Yeah.

00:42:45.629 --> 00:42:47.131
I mean, especially, I mean,

00:42:47.131 --> 00:42:48.465
I think big tech censorship
to me is that's just the,

00:42:48.465 --> 00:42:49.934
a really good example of this.

00:42:49.934 --> 00:42:53.671 align:start size:97% position:3%
There's this, in our
tradition, a wonderful right,

00:42:53.671 --> 00:42:56.807 align:start size:84% position:16%
like journalism holds
power to account.

00:42:56.807 --> 00:42:59.777 align:start size:94% position:6%
And now you have big
tech censoring, you know,

00:42:59.777 --> 00:43:01.278
the newspaper I worked for,

00:43:01.278 --> 00:43:05.149 align:start size:97% position:3%
New York Post, repeatedly last
year, for stories where we,

00:43:05.149 --> 00:43:07.318 align:start size:84% position:16%
on both accounts
we've been vindicated,

00:43:07.318 --> 00:43:11.055 align:start size:97% position:3%
Both the Wuhan lab-leak theory
and a Hunter Biden story,

00:43:11.055 --> 00:43:14.758
and a certain kind of
libertarian autonomy

00:43:14.758 --> 00:43:17.161
maximizing person
would say, well,

00:43:17.161 --> 00:43:18.996
it's private actors doing it.

00:43:18.996 --> 00:43:21.065
Who cares? Build
your own internet.

00:43:21.065 --> 00:43:23.467
But a humane person
would say, look,

00:43:23.467 --> 00:43:25.436
if these rights mean anything,

00:43:25.436 --> 00:43:27.771
then it's possible for
a private actor as much

00:43:27.771 --> 00:43:30.808
as the government
to violate them.

00:43:31.775 --> 00:43:33.210
- Yeah.

00:43:33.210 --> 00:43:35.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, the founders
could have never imagined

00:43:35.212 --> 00:43:39.083 align:start size:88% position:13%
a powerful entity, so
much that it would dwarf

00:43:39.083 --> 00:43:41.018 align:start size:84% position:16%
the entire government,
and here we are,

00:43:41.018 --> 00:43:42.486
that's why I've moved on this,

00:43:42.486 --> 00:43:46.924 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't make the purely
libertarian argument any more.

00:43:46.924 --> 00:43:48.826
- Michael Anton makes
his point beautifully

00:43:48.826 --> 00:43:50.060
in the June issue
of "First Things"

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:53.230
that they just didn't
foresee this degree

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:54.999
of private power.

00:43:54.999 --> 00:43:56.200
- They just couldn't have.

00:43:56.200 --> 00:43:57.601 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, I don't
think there's any way

00:43:57.601 --> 00:44:00.371
that they've could have
possibly imagined it.

00:44:00.371 --> 00:44:01.538
Question 10.

00:44:01.538 --> 00:44:03.040 align:start size:88% position:13%
I thought this was
interesting because now

00:44:03.040 --> 00:44:06.143 align:start size:97% position:3%
everything's public all the
time, is sex a private matter?

00:44:07.878 --> 00:44:08.712
- Yeah.

00:44:08.712 --> 00:44:10.114
I mean, look,

00:44:10.114 --> 00:44:13.751 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think we live in a
sexually schizophrenic age.

00:44:14.985 --> 00:44:18.222
In the one hand, you know,

00:44:18.222 --> 00:44:21.825 align:start size:97% position:3%
we're told to pursue whatever
we want sexually as long

00:44:21.825 --> 00:44:24.561 align:start size:88% position:13%
as we seek our partner's
consent and protect

00:44:24.561 --> 00:44:26.130
ourselves against disease.

00:44:26.130 --> 00:44:28.465
And that's the kind of
sex is just private fun.

00:44:28.465 --> 00:44:29.733
On the other hand,

00:44:29.733 --> 00:44:31.669
we live in the age of Me
Too where it seems like

00:44:31.669 --> 00:44:35.673
that sexual liberationism
in some cases has empowered

00:44:35.673 --> 00:44:38.575
some truly horrible men.

00:44:38.575 --> 00:44:41.979 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the focal point of the
chapter is someone who,

00:44:41.979 --> 00:44:43.347 align:start size:81% position:19%
a lot of people
will be surprised,

00:44:43.347 --> 00:44:45.249
isn't a book written
by a conservative,

00:44:45.249 --> 00:44:46.784
but it's Andrea Dworkin,

00:44:46.784 --> 00:44:50.287
who was a radical feminist,
really '70s, '80s, and '90s.

00:44:51.455 --> 00:44:54.124
And she was a critic of
the sexual revolution.

00:44:54.124 --> 00:44:56.226
I mean, she came
at it from the left

00:44:56.226 --> 00:44:58.896 align:start size:84% position:16%
and as she just
saw that, you know,

00:44:58.896 --> 00:45:01.732
sexual liberationism
had, in many ways,

00:45:01.732 --> 00:45:04.168 align:start size:84% position:16%
led to especially
degradation for women.

00:45:04.168 --> 00:45:09.173 align:start position:0%
And I argue that, in some
sense, she was a traditionalist,

00:45:10.708 --> 00:45:13.143 align:start size:88% position:13%
even though she would
hate to be in this book.

00:45:13.143 --> 00:45:14.378
If she was still alive,

00:45:14.378 --> 00:45:15.746
she would not wanna
be in a book next

00:45:15.746 --> 00:45:18.415
to Augustine and St. Thomas-

00:45:18.415 --> 00:45:20.684 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Now she definitely sounds
like a traditionalist,

00:45:20.684 --> 00:45:22.686
but then, not so much.

00:45:22.686 --> 00:45:23.787
- No, no.

00:45:23.787 --> 00:45:25.289
But I argue that, so look,

00:45:25.289 --> 00:45:28.192
the traditionalism of the
'80s where it was like, oh,

00:45:28.192 --> 00:45:31.962 align:start size:97% position:3%
all these feminists talking
about, well, you were

00:45:31.962 --> 00:45:34.732
celebrating a sexual
ethic that was only

00:45:34.732 --> 00:45:35.733
20 years old.

00:45:36.700 --> 00:45:39.536
And it was boorish and horrible.

00:45:40.738 --> 00:45:43.474
And there's nothing kind
of Christian about it.

00:45:43.474 --> 00:45:45.309
And there's nothing,

00:45:45.309 --> 00:45:48.245 align:start size:84% position:16%
it's only a relatively
recent vantage.

00:45:49.613 --> 00:45:53.784 align:start size:97% position:3%
And so the problem with
Dworkin was that she accepted

00:45:55.886 --> 00:45:59.022 align:start size:84% position:16%
this diagnosis of
what had gone wrong,

00:45:59.022 --> 00:46:01.759 align:start size:97% position:3%
or she proposed this diagnosis
of what had gone wrong

00:46:01.759 --> 00:46:03.894
with sexuality in the West,

00:46:03.894 --> 00:46:05.162 align:start size:88% position:13%
and what was wrong
with sex liberationism.

00:46:05.162 --> 00:46:08.031
But because she was
such a hard leftist,

00:46:08.031 --> 00:46:10.901
she couldn't look at
the older tradition

00:46:10.901 --> 00:46:14.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
of sexual restraint, because
that was just the right

00:46:14.371 --> 00:46:15.639
and it was bad.

00:46:17.107 --> 00:46:18.442
Yeah.

00:46:18.442 --> 00:46:20.477
- I get this from a lot
of my liberal friends,

00:46:20.477 --> 00:46:22.112
they get to the end of
the road and then the end

00:46:22.112 --> 00:46:23.847
of the road goes, oh, you know,

00:46:23.847 --> 00:46:25.582 align:start size:88% position:13%
some of the conservative
stuff was right.

00:46:25.582 --> 00:46:28.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
And that's where they suddenly
get very quiet, you know?

00:46:28.085 --> 00:46:29.987 align:start size:69% position:31%
- Exactly.
- Yeah.

00:46:29.987 --> 00:46:31.789
They don't wanna make
that final, uh-oh,

00:46:31.789 --> 00:46:34.958
maybe I really was wrong
about some bigger stuff.

00:46:34.958 --> 00:46:37.561 align:start size:94% position:6%
- That was her precisely,
because, I mean,

00:46:37.561 --> 00:46:40.998 align:start size:84% position:16%
all the chapters are
fairly uncritical

00:46:40.998 --> 00:46:42.866
of the figure profile.

00:46:42.866 --> 00:46:44.668
The Dworkin chapter,
I'm pretty critical

00:46:44.668 --> 00:46:48.205 align:start size:97% position:3%
of her and where I'm critical
is that she didn't see

00:46:48.205 --> 00:46:50.474 align:start size:94% position:6%
the shortcomings of her
own worldview, ultimately,

00:46:50.474 --> 00:46:53.844
and stopped short of going
all the way to look, well,

00:46:53.844 --> 00:46:55.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
maybe sexual
restraint was right.

00:46:55.879 --> 00:46:57.848
- Do you sense, that
that, right there,

00:46:57.848 --> 00:46:59.750
that inability to
get that last step,

00:46:59.750 --> 00:47:03.720 align:start size:97% position:3%
that that's more of a
condition of a liberal mind,

00:47:03.720 --> 00:47:04.721
something like that?

00:47:07.825 --> 00:47:09.159
- Well, I mean, I wanna be fair.

00:47:09.159 --> 00:47:12.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think it's a
shortcoming for all of us,

00:47:12.596 --> 00:47:15.098
where your worldview takes you,

00:47:15.098 --> 00:47:17.367
you may stop short
of going all the way

00:47:17.367 --> 00:47:19.269
because it's uncomfortable.

00:47:19.269 --> 00:47:20.470
- Yeah.

00:47:20.470 --> 00:47:23.607 align:start size:88% position:13%
- It's worthwhile to
try to avoid that. Yeah.

00:47:23.607 --> 00:47:24.842
- Exactly.

00:47:24.842 --> 00:47:27.711
- Question 11. What
do you owe your body?

00:47:27.711 --> 00:47:29.146
Which I thought was
really interesting,

00:47:29.146 --> 00:47:31.381 align:start size:84% position:16%
because we talk a
lot about, you know,

00:47:31.381 --> 00:47:33.617 align:start size:84% position:16%
your body your
choice, and now it's,

00:47:33.617 --> 00:47:36.720 align:start size:97% position:3%
everybody's gotta get jabbed
and everybody's bodies,

00:47:36.720 --> 00:47:39.156
everybody or we're gonna
kill grandma, et cetera.

00:47:40.524 --> 00:47:42.159
- I mean, the chapter
is really a critique

00:47:42.159 --> 00:47:43.794
of modern Gnosticism.

00:47:44.661 --> 00:47:45.996
In the ancient world,

00:47:45.996 --> 00:47:48.699
in 200, 300 years
after the advent

00:47:48.699 --> 00:47:51.835 align:start size:97% position:3%
of Christianity a whole
bunch of religions popped up

00:47:51.835 --> 00:47:53.637
that were unique in the
sense that they said

00:47:53.637 --> 00:47:57.074
that human person
is not the bodily.

00:47:57.074 --> 00:48:00.611 align:start size:97% position:3%
You are this kind of
immaterial spirit that happens

00:48:00.611 --> 00:48:03.947
to be trapped in this
fleshly apparatus,

00:48:03.947 --> 00:48:06.617 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you can do whatever
you want with it.

00:48:06.617 --> 00:48:09.820 align:start size:84% position:16%
The body itself
doesn't have a norm.

00:48:12.322 --> 00:48:15.959
What you should try to do
is to liberate this divine

00:48:15.959 --> 00:48:18.295
spark that's trapped within you.

00:48:18.295 --> 00:48:20.998 align:start size:88% position:13%
And that might sound
familiar to some moderns

00:48:20.998 --> 00:48:24.301 align:start size:97% position:3%
because many modern movements
have the same tendency.

00:48:25.669 --> 00:48:27.771 align:start size:97% position:3%
Transgenderism, which
we've already talked about,

00:48:27.771 --> 00:48:30.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
is premised on the idea
that there's this dramatic

00:48:30.941 --> 00:48:34.745
rupture between who we
feel we are interiorly

00:48:34.745 --> 00:48:37.781 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the bodies that we receive
from nature which are sexed

00:48:37.781 --> 00:48:40.250
and you're either
male or female.

00:48:40.250 --> 00:48:43.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
Transhumanism is
another example of this.

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:45.455
There's movements
that think, look,

00:48:45.455 --> 00:48:48.025
the human being
is just the mind.

00:48:48.025 --> 00:48:51.595
And so if I can download
the mind into some kind

00:48:51.595 --> 00:48:54.898 align:start size:97% position:3%
of software form and
untether what it means to be

00:48:54.898 --> 00:48:56.466
human from this, again,

00:48:56.466 --> 00:49:00.170
this body that's prone to
decrepitude and disease

00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:03.573
and ultimately death,
then that's liberation.

00:49:03.573 --> 00:49:06.543
But the problem with all
these kinds of movements

00:49:06.543 --> 00:49:11.214 align:start size:97% position:3%
is that they're an invitation
to, not only unreality,

00:49:11.214 --> 00:49:14.251
because our bodies
are just ultimately

00:49:14.251 --> 00:49:18.088 align:start position:0%
a part of us, but also an
invitation to irresponsibility.

00:49:20.357 --> 00:49:23.994
If I'm me, I'm not the person,

00:49:23.994 --> 00:49:25.429
including this body,

00:49:25.429 --> 00:49:27.931 align:start size:97% position:3%
then I don't have all
these inherited obligations

00:49:27.931 --> 00:49:30.434
to family, to the people
who bore me and gave me

00:49:30.434 --> 00:49:33.603
the shape of my body,
to my larger community,

00:49:33.603 --> 00:49:36.406 align:start size:97% position:3%
because I'm the sort of
spirit creature and I can go,

00:49:36.406 --> 00:49:39.843
I can use Bitcoin, I
can use virtual reality

00:49:39.843 --> 00:49:41.411
and I don't need
physical contact,

00:49:41.411 --> 00:49:43.580 align:start size:84% position:16%
I don't need to
shake anyone's hand.

00:49:43.580 --> 00:49:45.682
And actually I think the
lockdowns kind of have

00:49:45.682 --> 00:49:47.150
a Gnostic tendency, by the way.

00:49:47.150 --> 00:49:49.786
You know, people would
just say, well, its just,

00:49:49.786 --> 00:49:52.589
universities should just
be an online phenomenon.

00:49:52.589 --> 00:49:55.058 align:start size:97% position:3%
We should always wear a
masks because, you know what,

00:49:55.058 --> 00:49:56.593
we don't need to
see their faces.

00:49:56.593 --> 00:49:59.963 align:start size:97% position:3%
And even if we've conquered,
COVID there are other,

00:50:01.431 --> 00:50:02.599
there's the flu.

00:50:02.599 --> 00:50:04.067 align:start size:84% position:16%
We could always
conquer the flu, too.

00:50:04.067 --> 00:50:07.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that, I mean, all
these tendencies, I argue,

00:50:07.871 --> 00:50:11.174 align:start size:94% position:6%
trace back to this ancient
heresy of Gnosticism.

00:50:11.174 --> 00:50:14.611
- Yeah. Well, they're
deeply human needs.

00:50:14.611 --> 00:50:16.413 align:start size:84% position:16%
A couple of times
during the lockdowns,

00:50:16.413 --> 00:50:19.516 align:start size:97% position:3%
I've done meet and greets here
in LA, which, I don't, now,

00:50:19.516 --> 00:50:21.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
I guess, there're
semi-legal, I'm not sure,

00:50:21.351 --> 00:50:22.853
but when I've met people,

00:50:22.853 --> 00:50:24.721
people literally crying
because they haven't seen

00:50:24.721 --> 00:50:26.189
humans for that long.

00:50:26.189 --> 00:50:27.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
They haven't seen someone
stand there and smile

00:50:27.958 --> 00:50:29.326
and have a beer.

00:50:29.326 --> 00:50:31.762
And it's like, that's
deep. We need that.

00:50:31.762 --> 00:50:35.332
- That's so sad. But
we're human, yeah.

00:50:35.332 --> 00:50:37.434 align:start size:75% position:25%
- But it's true.
- Yeah.

00:50:37.434 --> 00:50:41.638
- The final question. This is
the final question, I suppose.

00:50:41.638 --> 00:50:43.540
What's good about death?

00:50:46.209 --> 00:50:49.546
- Look, I wrote that chapter
at the height of the pandemic,

00:50:49.546 --> 00:50:54.351 align:start size:88% position:13%
and it's focused on
Seneca, the great Roman

00:50:54.351 --> 00:50:59.089 align:start size:94% position:6%
stoic philosopher
statesman, who argued that

00:51:00.424 --> 00:51:04.628 align:start position:0%
life becomes kind of
meaningless if you live forever.

00:51:04.628 --> 00:51:07.097 align:start size:84% position:16%
And that it's not
that he was said to be

00:51:07.097 --> 00:51:10.167
a foolhardy jackass and
like jump down 10 floors

00:51:10.167 --> 00:51:12.369
and expect to live.

00:51:12.369 --> 00:51:14.004
But to live in,

00:51:14.004 --> 00:51:16.239
always in fear of death

00:51:19.609 --> 00:51:20.811
prevents you from having

00:51:20.811 --> 00:51:23.447 align:start size:84% position:16%
the right perspective
on your life.

00:51:23.447 --> 00:51:24.948
You hoard your possessions,

00:51:24.948 --> 00:51:29.019
you are fearful of
new experiences, you,

00:51:29.019 --> 00:51:30.687 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's all sorts of things
that you do if you sort

00:51:30.687 --> 00:51:33.590 align:start size:84% position:16%
of are just absolutely
fearful of death.

00:51:33.590 --> 00:51:35.859
Whereas if you keep it in
a rational perspective,

00:51:35.859 --> 00:51:37.994
and say, look, I'm gonna die.

00:51:37.994 --> 00:51:41.364
It actually liberates
you to act, you know,

00:51:41.364 --> 00:51:44.935
boldly in life and as
he did in his life.

00:51:46.403 --> 00:51:50.373
But ultimately also that a
life in which you don't have

00:51:50.373 --> 00:51:52.242 align:start size:84% position:16%
an end point is
kind of meandering.

00:51:52.242 --> 00:51:54.111
It's like a novel that
doesn't have a beginning,

00:51:54.111 --> 00:51:55.645
middle and an end.

00:51:55.645 --> 00:51:57.881
And those kinds of novels
or movies are terrible.

00:51:59.015 --> 00:52:01.918 align:start size:88% position:13%
All the things we
value in life, heroism,

00:52:01.918 --> 00:52:04.855
sacrifice, true beauty.

00:52:04.855 --> 00:52:07.991
They all find their meaning
in relation to an endpoint.

00:52:07.991 --> 00:52:12.028 align:start size:97% position:3%
If you could live forever,
they're all kind of cheapened.

00:52:12.028 --> 00:52:14.865
And, now, obviously a
Christian or a faithful

00:52:14.865 --> 00:52:16.566 align:start size:84% position:16%
Jew or Muslim wouldn't
agree with him,

00:52:16.566 --> 00:52:18.802 align:start size:88% position:13%
because he would say
there's an eternal life,

00:52:18.802 --> 00:52:20.904
but they would agree with
him in so far as saying,

00:52:20.904 --> 00:52:24.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
with Seneca, that is,
that this version of life,

00:52:24.941 --> 00:52:28.011 align:start position:0%
of trying to bio-technologically
enhance life forever,

00:52:28.011 --> 00:52:31.148 align:start position:0%
that's not eternal life, that's
a kind of fake substitute.

00:52:31.148 --> 00:52:33.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I think that chapter
resonates with a lot

00:52:33.483 --> 00:52:35.352
of people because of, frankly,

00:52:35.352 --> 00:52:36.720
the COVID restrictions again.

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:37.854
- Yeah.

00:52:37.854 --> 00:52:39.289 align:start size:88% position:13%
It must've been
fascinating just writing

00:52:39.289 --> 00:52:41.191 align:start size:97% position:3%
that in the midst of COVID,
where it went from two

00:52:41.191 --> 00:52:43.727
weeks to flatten the curve
to now we're locking down

00:52:43.727 --> 00:52:46.630
for a year to then
rolling lockdowns.

00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:48.064
And then suddenly it's like, oh,

00:52:48.064 --> 00:52:51.668 align:start size:97% position:3%
we can't open up until there's
zero chance for anybody

00:52:51.668 --> 00:52:54.838 align:start size:97% position:3%
to get it, which is actually
statistically impossible.

00:52:54.838 --> 00:52:56.373
- And double masking

00:52:56.373 --> 00:52:59.242 align:start size:88% position:13%
which is my least
favorite study. (laughs)

00:52:59.242 --> 00:53:00.844
- And double masking
and everything else.

00:53:00.844 --> 00:53:03.213
Let me ask you one other
thing that's not in this,

00:53:03.213 --> 00:53:07.417 align:start size:97% position:3%
you did not pose the
question, but are you hopeful?

00:53:07.417 --> 00:53:10.187
That seems to be coming up a
lot with a lot of my guests.

00:53:10.187 --> 00:53:11.888 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm trying to figure
out who's hopeful

00:53:11.888 --> 00:53:14.057
for the future and why?

00:53:14.057 --> 00:53:16.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
Are you hopeful that we
can turn this thing around?

00:53:16.393 --> 00:53:18.728 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because a lot of the questions
that you pose here are

00:53:18.728 --> 00:53:21.131 align:start position:0%
not things that we're grappling
with in a public sense

00:53:21.131 --> 00:53:22.365
in any real way.

00:53:22.365 --> 00:53:24.034
I mean, obviously,
certain people are,

00:53:24.034 --> 00:53:25.535
the book's are doing well,

00:53:25.535 --> 00:53:27.704
and we talk about these things
